      "problem": "Evaluate $\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^3.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $-\\dfrac{3}{2}=\\dfrac{(-3)}{2}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^3=\\left(\\dfrac{(-3)}{2}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{(-3)^3}{2^3}\n$$\n\nNow, computing $(-3)^3=-27$ and $2^3=8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(-3)^3}{2^3}= \\dfrac{-27}{8}=-\\dfrac{27}{8} .\n$$",
